[38;5;231m⏺[39m Browser-local it is.

  Next fork: [1mwhat actually gets delivered.[0m The app has no settings today, so "preferences storage" could mean just the
  store, or the store plus something visibly using it. This matters because a store with no consumer has an unvalidated
  interface — you don't find out the API is wrong until the first real preference lands on it.

  - [1mStore module only[0m — a [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m with get/set/remove/defaults over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, plus tests. Smallest surface,
    but nothing in the app reads it yet, so the API is a guess.
  - [1mStore + one preference wired end to end[0m — the same module, plus a single real preference proving the path from UI to
    storage and back on reload. My pick: it validates the interface at nearly no extra cost, and the second preference
    becomes trivial once the first works.
  - [1mStore + a settings panel[0m — adds a settings UI section to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with several controls. Largest scope; worth it
    only if you know you want a settings screen now.

  One constraint I'll hold regardless of your answer, since this is a login form: [1mno password is ever written to [0m
  [1mstorage.[0m A "remember me" style preference persists the username only. Persisting a credential to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m exposes
  it to any script on the page, and I won't design that in without you explicitly asking.
